<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 2.1 Infinite Series.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, so
  the notes can be completed by hand (e.g. on an iPad over the PDF, or
  printed).  Content mirrors ch-10-2.ptx of the full lecture notes;
  every xml:id here is prefixed with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: the unit-square picture is a simple shape, so only blank
  space is left for the student to draw it.  The bouncing-ball figure
  is reproduced in full, as in the lecture notes.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-series-infinite" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Infinite Series</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Define an infinite sequence, an infinite series, and the sequence of
partial sums, and decide convergence of a series from its partial sums.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Recognize a geometric series, and determine whether it converges and
what it converges to.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Apply the <m>n</m>th Term Test for divergence, and understand what the
test can and cannot conclude.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Combine convergent series using the sum, difference, and constant
multiple rules, and evaluate telescoping series from their partial
sums.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
What does it mean to add infinitely many numbers together? In this
section we answer that question by defining an infinite series and its
sequence of partial sums, so that convergence of a series becomes a
statement about a limit we already know how to study. We then put the
definition to work on the geometric series, whose behavior we can settle
completely, and on the <m>n</m>th Term Test, which is our first quick
check for divergence.
</p>
<p>
<em>These are skeletal notes: the definitions, theorems, and problem
statements are given, and blank boxes are left wherever a derivation, a
solution, or a sketch belongs, so that you can fill them in by hand.</em>
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-series-definitions">
<title>Definitions</title>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-sequence">
<title>Sequence</title>
<statement>
<p>
A <term>sequence</term> is a list of numbers,
<m>a_1, a_2, a_3, \cdots</m>. An <term>infinite sequence</term> of
numbers is a function whose domain is the set of positive integers.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="example-skel-series-halving-sequence">
<title>A Sequence of Halves</title>
<statement>
<p>
The numbers
<m>\dfrac12, \dfrac14, \dfrac18, \dfrac{1}{16}, \cdots</m> form an
infinite sequence; its <m>n</m>th term is
<m>a_n = \left(\dfrac12\right)^{n}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-infinite-series">
<title>Infinite Series</title>
<statement>
<p>
The sum of the numbers in an infinite sequence <m>\{a_n\}</m>, i.e.
<m>a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \cdots = \displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n</m>,
is called an <term>infinite series</term>. Here <m>a_n</m> is the
<m>n</m>th term of the series.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="example-skel-series-halving-series">
<title>An Infinite Series with a Finite Sum</title>
<statement>
<p>
Infinite sequences can have finite sums. Consider the sum of the
sequence from <xref ref="example-skel-series-halving-sequence"/>, i.e.
<m>\dfrac12 + \dfrac14 + \dfrac18 + \dfrac{1}{16} + \cdots</m>.
</p>
<p>
It is most convenient to evaluate the result of this sum geometrically.
Draw a square of side one in the space below. Shade half of it, then
half of what is left, then half of what is left after that, and keep
going. Label the pieces <m>\frac12</m>, <m>\frac14</m>, <m>\frac18</m>,
<m>\frac{1}{16}</m>. What is the total shaded area, and therefore what
is the sum?
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {draw the unit square and its subdivisions here};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank space in which to draw a unit square subdivided into rectangles of area one half, one fourth, one eighth, one sixteenth, and so on.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-series-partial-sums">
<title>Partial Sums</title>

<p>
Consider again the infinite sequence
<m>\dfrac12, \dfrac14, \dfrac18, \dfrac{1}{16}, \cdots</m>. Let us
denote the sum of the first <m>n</m> terms in this sequence by
<m>s_n</m>, which are known as <term>partial sums</term>. Can we find a
pattern in the sequence of partial sums?
</p>

<p>
Write out <m>s_1</m>, <m>s_2</m>, and <m>s_3</m>, each one both as a
fraction and in the form <m>1 - \square</m>. Then guess a closed formula
for <m>s_n = \displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left(\frac12\right)^{k}</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,7.1) {$s_1 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,5.6) {$s_2 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,4.1) {$s_3 =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,2.6) {$\vdots$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,1.5) {$s_n =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with rows labelled s sub 1, s sub 2, s sub 3, and s sub n, for computing the partial sums by hand.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Now use your formula for <m>s_n</m> to compute the infinite series, by
taking the limit of the partial sum as <m>n \to \infty</m>. Compare the
answer with the one you found geometrically from the square.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for taking the limit of the partial sums by hand.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
In the example above, the sequence of partial sums converged to a
number. In general, if the sequence of the partial sums converges to a
number, we say that the series <term>converges</term>, otherwise we say
that the series <term>diverges</term>.
</p>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-series-geometric">
<title>Geometric Series</title>

<p>
An important example of infinite series is the geometric series. The
<term>geometric series</term> is of the form
<md xml:id="eq-skel-series-geometric" number="yes">
a + ar + ar^2 + \cdots + ar^{n-1} + \cdots
= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} ar^{n-1},
</md>
where <m>a, r</m> are real numbers and <m>a \ne 0</m>. Note that we can
re-write the series as <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} ar^{n}</m>.
</p>

<p>
Let us compute the partial sum <m>s_n</m> for the geometric series, so
that
<md xml:id="eq-skel-series-geo-sn" number="yes">
s_n = a + ar + ar^2 + \cdots + ar^{n-1}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Multiply <xref ref="eq-skel-series-geo-sn"/> by <m>r</m>, subtract the
result from <xref ref="eq-skel-series-geo-sn"/>, and solve for
<m>s_n</m>. Almost every term should cancel.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,6.6) {$r\,s_n =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,5.1) {$s_n - r\,s_n =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.35,3.0) {$s_n =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with rows labelled r times s sub n, s sub n minus r times s sub n, and s sub n, for deriving the partial sum of a geometric series.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
Next, take the limit of your partial sum as <m>n \to \infty</m>. Treat
the cases <m>|r| \lt 1</m>, <m>|r| \gt 1</m>, and <m>r = 1</m>
separately, and in each case say what
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} r^{n}</m> does.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.75) {$|r| &lt; 1$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.75) {$|r| &gt; 1$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.75) {$r = 1$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with three rows labelled absolute value of r less than one, greater than one, and r equals one, for the three cases of the geometric series.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<exercise xml:id="skel-checkpoint-series-geometric-r-negative-one">
<title>What Happens When <m>r = -1</m>?</title>
<statement>
<p>
We have now handled <m>|r| \lt 1</m>, <m>|r| \gt 1</m>, and
<m>r = 1</m>, but one case is still missing: <m>r = -1</m>. Write out the
partial sums <m>s_1, s_2, s_3, s_4</m> of the series
<m>a - a + a - a + \cdots = \displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a(-1)^{n-1}</m>
for <m>a \neq 0</m>. Does the sequence of partial sums approach a single
number as <m>n \to \infty</m>? What does that tell you about the series?
</p>

<p>
<em>Your answer and reasoning.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten answer.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
The partial sums do not grow without bound here, the way they do when
<m>r = 1</m>. Look instead at whether they settle down to one value.
</p>
</hint>
</exercise>

<p>
Putting all of the cases together, we can say that if <m>|r| \ge 1</m>,
the geometric series is divergent.
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-summary-geometric-series">
<title>Summary</title>
<p>
The geometric series <xref ref="eq-skel-series-geometric"/> converges to
<m>\dfrac{a}{1-r}</m> if <m>|r| \lt 1</m>, i.e.
<md xml:id="eq-skel-series-geometric-sum" number="yes">
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} ar^{n-1} = \frac{a}{1-r},
\qquad |r| \lt 1,
</md>
and diverges if <m>|r| \ge 1</m>.
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Next, as an application of the geometric series, we will go through the
following example, which is from our textbook.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-skel-series-ball">
<title>A bouncing ball</title>
<statement>
<p>
You drop a ball from <m>a</m> meters above a flat surface. Each time
the ball hits the surface after falling a distance <m>h</m>, it
rebounds a distance <m>rh</m>, where <m>r</m> is positive but less than
1. Find the total distance the ball travels up and down.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Use <xref ref="fig-skel-series-ball"/> to write the total distance as
an infinite series, then match it against
<xref ref="eq-skel-series-geometric-sum"/>. Watch the first drop: it is
travelled only once.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,8.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten solution.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-series-ball">
<caption>
The ball falls a distance <m>a</m>, then rises and falls a distance
<m>ar</m>, then <m>ar^2</m>, and so on, so the total distance travelled
is <m>a + 2ar + 2ar^2 + \cdots</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-series-ball">
<diagram dimensions="(340,280)" margins="12">
<definition>b0(t) = (0.55 + 0.55*t, 4.0*(1 - t^2))</definition>
<definition>b1(t) = (1.10 + 0.9*t, 2.4*(4*t*(1 - t)))</definition>
<definition>b2(t) = (2.00 + 0.7*t, 1.44*(4*t*(1 - t)))</definition>
<definition>b3(t) = (2.70 + 0.55*t, 0.864*(4*t*(1 - t)))</definition>
<definition>b4(t) = (3.25 + 0.42*t, 0.5184*(4*t*(1 - t)))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.7,-0.5,4.4,4.6)">
<line at="ball-ground" endpoints="((-0.35,0),(4.2,0))"
      stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<line at="ball-wall" endpoints="((-0.35,0),(-0.35,4.4))"
      stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<line at="ball-lev1" endpoints="((-0.35,2.4),(2.0,2.4))"
      stroke="#D6336C" dash="5 4" thickness="1"/>
<line at="ball-lev2" endpoints="((-0.35,1.44),(2.7,1.44))"
      stroke="#D6336C" dash="5 4" thickness="1"/>
<line at="ball-lev3" endpoints="((-0.35,0.864),(3.25,0.864))"
      stroke="#D6336C" dash="5 4" thickness="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="ball-c0" function="b0" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ball-c1" function="b1" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ball-c2" function="b2" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ball-c3" function="b3" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ball-c4" function="b4" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="ball-start" p="(0.55,4.0)" fill="#56B4E9"/>
<label at="ball-a" anchor="(-0.45,4.0)" alignment="w">
<m>a</m>
</label>
<label at="ball-ar" anchor="(-0.45,2.4)" alignment="w">
<m>ar</m>
</label>
<label at="ball-ar2" anchor="(-0.45,1.44)" alignment="w">
<m>ar^2</m>
</label>
<label at="ball-ar3" anchor="(-0.45,0.864)" alignment="w">
<m>ar^3</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A ball dropped from height a follows a sequence of bounces that decay geometrically. Dashed horizontal lines mark the rebound heights a r, a r squared, and a r cubed, each a fixed fraction of the previous height.">
  <annotation ref="ball-c0" text="The initial drop from height a to the ground."/>
  <annotation ref="ball-c1" text="The first bounce, rising to height a r and falling back."/>
  <annotation ref="ball-c2" text="The second bounce, rising to height a r squared."/>
  <annotation ref="ball-c3" text="The third bounce, rising to height a r cubed. The bounces continue forever, but the total distance is finite."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-series-nth-term">
<title>The <m>n</m>th Term Test</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-series-nth-term">
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n</m> converges, then
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
<term>Important note:</term> If <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n = 0</m>,
we <em>cannot</em> conclude that
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n</m> converges. See parts D and E
in the example below.
</p>

<p>
The following test is a consequence of the above theorem.
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="skel-test-nth-term">
<title>The <m>n</m>th Term Test</title>
<p>
If <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n</m> does not exist or
<m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n \ne 0</m>, then
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n</m> diverges.
</p>
</assemblage>

<example xml:id="example-skel-series-convergence">
<title>Testing series for convergence</title>
<statement>
<p>
Determine whether the series is convergent or divergent. If it is
convergent, find its sum.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1)^n</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{n^3+1}{n^2+n}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(n+1)}</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\dfrac49 - \dfrac{8}{27} + \dfrac{16}{81} - \dfrac{32}{243} + \cdots</m>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(For each one, start by computing <m>\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} a_n</m>.
For D, the <m>n</m>th Term Test will not settle it, so write the partial
sum instead and use
<m>\dfrac{1}{k(k+1)} = \dfrac1k - \dfrac{1}{k+1}</m>. For E, the test
will not settle it either, so write the series in sigma notation and
identify <m>a</m> and <m>r</m>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,17) -- (15,17);
\draw[gray!35] (0,14) -- (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,11) -- (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,19.75) {A.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,16.75) {B.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.75) {C.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.75) {D.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.25) {E.};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box divided into five rows labelled A through E, for testing each of the five series by hand.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-series-combining">
<title>Combining Series</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-series-combining">
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\sum a_n = A</m> and <m>\sum b_n = B</m> are convergent series,
then

<ol>
<li>
<p>
<em>Sum Rule:</em>
<m>\sum (a_n + b_n) = \sum a_n + \sum b_n = A + B</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>Difference Rule:</em>
<m>\sum (a_n - b_n) = \sum a_n - \sum b_n = A - B</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>Constant Multiple Rule:</em>
<m>\sum k a_n = k \sum a_n = kA</m> (Any number <m>k</m>).
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<example xml:id="example-skel-series-difference">
<title>Using the difference rule</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\frac{1}{n(n+1)} - \frac{1}{2^n}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(You have already summed both of these series in this section: one in
<xref ref="subsec-skel-series-partial-sums"/> and the other in part D of
<xref ref="example-skel-series-convergence"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten solution.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-skel-series-telescoping-ln">
<title>A telescoping series with logarithms</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
\ln\!\left(\frac{k+2}{k+1}\right)</m>, if it converges and if it
diverges, show that it does.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Begin by writing the partial sum <m>s_n</m>, and use
<m>\ln\!\left(\frac{k+2}{k+1}\right) = \ln(k+2) - \ln(k+1)</m> so that
the sum telescopes.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,8.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten solution.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>
</subsection>

</section>
